        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Powering the Agent Development Lifecycle with any framework, from ADK to LangChain and beyond]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[The Agents CLI Extension System enables developers to use frameworks like LangChain, LangGraph, or custom enterprise stacks while retaining a unified deployment and evaluation workflow. It details a first-party LangChain template that scaffolds, runs, and deploys agents without requiring the Agent Development Kit (ADK). Additionally, it demonstrates how platform teams can author custom extensions to enforce policies such as automated software bill of materials (SBOM) scans during deployment.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/powering-the-agent-development-lifecycle-with-any-framework-from-adk-to-langchain-and-beyond/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/powering-the-agent-development-lifecycle-with-any-framework-from-adk-to-langchain-and-beyond/</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 27 Mar 2020 13:07:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Bringing Visual Graph Debugging to Custom Edge Compilers with Model Explorer]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[Google AI Edge Model Explorer now supports custom compiler intermediate representations and open-source adapters like TOSA, VGF, and PTE for on-device machine learning. Developers can leverage hierarchical graph visualization, split-pane views, and per-node trace overlays to catch export regressions and verify hardware delegation. These tools enable engineering teams to transparently debug execution graphs and pinpoint cycle-level latency bottlenecks locally.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/bringing-visual-graph-debugging-to-custom-edge-compilers-with-model-explorer/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/bringing-visual-graph-debugging-to-custom-edge-compilers-with-model-explorer/</guid>
            <pubDate>Sat, 24 Oct 2020 22:13:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Integrating TorchTPU into TorchTitan and Optimizing Models for TPU]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[TorchTPU integrates a native torch.device("tpu") backend directly into PyTorch, allowing developers to train large-scale models on Google Cloud TPUs without rewriting code in JAX. The TorchTitan TPU fork enhances this with production-grade scaling primitives like SimpleFSDP, Graph Trainer, and custom Pallas kernels that achieve high Model Flops Utilization (MFU) across dense and MoE architectures. Developers can leverage these battle-tested optimization playbooks, recipes, and the Hugging Face modeling backend to efficiently scale their own workloads on TPU infrastructure.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/integrating-torchtpu-into-torchtitan-and-optimizing-models-for-tpu/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/integrating-torchtpu-into-torchtitan-and-optimizing-models-for-tpu/</guid>
            <pubDate>Wed, 10 Jun 2020 12:38:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Announcing New vLLM and SGLang Inference Engines on TPU]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[This post announces native PyTorch backends for vLLM and SGLang powered by TorchTPU, enabling developers to run and optimize serving workloads using familiar PyTorch tools. Built in collaboration with open-source project maintainers, these engines include optimized TPU kernel implementations, production-ready features like KV cache management with TPU Sync, and disaggregated serving. Public benchmarks demonstrate state-of-the-art performance and industry-leading cost efficiency on Ironwood TPU across long-context and agentic workloads.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/announcing-new-vllm-and-sglang-inference-engines-on-tpu/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/announcing-new-vllm-and-sglang-inference-engines-on-tpu/</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 21 Apr 2020 17:56:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Unifying Edge AI: How Coral NPU’s open standard brings portable, energy-viable intelligence from model to silicon]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[Google Research has completed the 32-bit open-source Coral NPU RISC-V architecture with the introduction of its Matrix Execution Unit, which delivers up to 1 TOPS while targeting 10 mW average power for edge inference. Alongside this architecture, the release of the v1.0 open-source IREE/MLIR compiler allows developers to take models from JAX, PyTorch, and TensorFlow straight to Coral NPU hardware without writing custom assembly. Developers can now prototype and deploy these multimodal edge applications using new hardware options like the Synaptics Astra SR200 MCU series and globally available Coralboard devkits.12]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/unifying-edge-ai-how-coral-npus-open-standard-brings-portable-energy-viable-intelligence-from-model-to-silicon/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/unifying-edge-ai-how-coral-npus-open-standard-brings-portable-energy-viable-intelligence-from-model-to-silicon/</guid>
            <pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2020 22:07:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
